73
00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:25.560
Andy Woodruff: So…

74
00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:36.159
Andy Woodruff: Let's see… Good morning, everyone, and good evening. I'm Andy Woodruff, the Executive Director of the Open Commercial Media Ecosystem, or OCME.

75
00:08:36.289 --> 00:08:48.040
Andy Woodruff: Before we start, I just have a quick disclaimer here that this is a 30-minute presentation, and I could not fit everything in in full detail. Some of the individual slides that I have

76
00:08:48.040 --> 00:08:57.390
Andy Woodruff: Could be an entire presentation on their own, so there will be a QR code at the end, and if you scan that, we can connect and talk more.

77
00:08:58.300 --> 00:09:05.889
Andy Woodruff: Here at the beginning, I want to set the groundwork, providing a quick overview of the OCME and creator-governed content.

78
00:09:06.090 --> 00:09:13.479
Andy Woodruff: But ultimately, the main focus of this presentation is on our ecosystem structure and our governance structure.

79
00:09:13.580 --> 00:09:21.269
Andy Woodruff: I want to not only inform you about what we are doing, but also gather your questions and feedback so we can refine our work.

80
00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:35.149
Andy Woodruff: The Open Commercial Media Ecosystem is a non-profit, members-based organization founded in 2024 to restructure how digital content is valued, shared, and monetized.

81
00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:42.619
Andy Woodruff: We want to return to a community-led space where creating, collaborating, and sharing your art is in your control.

82
00:09:43.030 --> 00:09:45.539
Andy Woodruff: An era of creator-governed content.

83
00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:50.999
Andy Woodruff: To reach a global audience to define how digital media is controlled and consumed.

84
00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:55.889
Andy Woodruff: The global creator market size.

85
00:09:56.080 --> 00:10:04.980
Andy Woodruff: is estimated at $205 billion in 2024, and projected to reach $1.3 trillion by 2033.

86
00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:10.299
Andy Woodruff: Yet, the fundamental relationship between creators and platforms remains broken.

87
00:10:10.630 --> 00:10:17.510
Andy Woodruff: Platforms exercise unilateral control over monetization, content policies, and account access.

88
00:10:17.880 --> 00:10:25.419
Andy Woodruff: Platforms create lock-in through network effects and winner-take-all dynamics, extracting data as the new resource.

89
00:10:25.630 --> 00:10:32.459
Andy Woodruff: While the creator economy displaced traditional gatekeepers, like record labels, publishers, and studios.

90
00:10:32.770 --> 00:10:40.860
Andy Woodruff: Platforms have inherited the same power dynamic. Control over creator identity means control over creator economics.

91
00:10:41.850 --> 00:10:55.850
Andy Woodruff: Working within these existing platforms and ecosystems is an incredibly daunting challenge that requires incredible financing and powerful connections. Self-sovereign identity, blockchain, and AI have changed the equation.

92
00:10:55.960 --> 00:11:05.559
Andy Woodruff: These three technologies eliminate traditional cost centers, dramatically reducing the financing and powerful connections needed to build outside the existing system.

93
00:11:05.890 --> 00:11:09.729
Andy Woodruff: So that more value flows directly to the creators.

94
00:11:12.230 --> 00:11:18.069
Andy Woodruff: We're making the fundamental shift from user-generated content to creator-governed content.

95
00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:20.219
Andy Woodruff: So let me explain the difference.

96
00:11:20.550 --> 00:11:29.589
Andy Woodruff: With user-generated content, the platform owns your identity. The platform controls the policies, which will change at any time without your input.

97
00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:40.120
Andy Woodruff: The platform sets the revenue rates, and in this model, you're dealing with shareholders. The platform is looking out for them, not the creators or the subscribers.

98
00:11:40.510 --> 00:11:43.700
Andy Woodruff: In contrast, with creator-governed content.

99
00:11:43.770 --> 00:12:01.170
Andy Woodruff: The user controls their identity. The users control policies as voting members of the governance structure. Users set revenue rates through democratic processes, and critically, you're dealing with stakeholders. Those involved in the value creation get a say.

100
00:12:01.930 --> 00:12:08.460
Andy Woodruff: This isn't just a terminology… change in terminology, it's a fundamental restructuring of the power dynamics.

101
00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:24.910
Andy Woodruff: In UGC, you're subject to platform decisions. In CGC, you participate in making those decisions. The transition from shareholder to stakeholder means moving from passive participant to active governor.

102
00:12:26.850 --> 00:12:31.660
Andy Woodruff: This quote on self-sovereign identity captures the philosophical foundation.

103
00:12:32.190 --> 00:12:39.259
Andy Woodruff: A person's identity that is neither dependent on nor subject to any other power or state.

104
00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:45.540
Andy Woodruff: Today, most of the progress I see comes dictated from governments or large corporations.

105
00:12:45.700 --> 00:12:56.749
Andy Woodruff: Government-mandated identification can be compatible with SSI infrastructure, but OCME's bottom-up approach embraces the full philosophy.

106
00:12:57.370 --> 00:13:02.650
Andy Woodruff: Identity that individuals control because they choose to, not because they're required to.

107
00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:15.229
Andy Woodruff: An individual can join OCME of their own free will, with the goal of receiving more value by being part of the ecosystem than being outside of it. There's no government mandate, no employer requirement.

108
00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:19.400
Andy Woodruff: Members join because they want to be a part of the ecosystem.

109
00:13:19.670 --> 00:13:24.129
Andy Woodruff: This philosophical commitment shapes everything about how we've built the system.

110
00:13:25.140 --> 00:13:29.109
Andy Woodruff: Now let's take a look at the technical elegance that makes this possible.

111
00:13:32.450 --> 00:13:36.740
Andy Woodruff: We have efficiency through technology convergence.

112
00:13:36.980 --> 00:13:40.429
Andy Woodruff: Starting with SSI, every creator gets a DID.

113
00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:47.389
Andy Woodruff: Every piece of content gets a DID. Every playlist, every… and split sheet and play record gets a DID.

114
00:13:47.680 --> 00:13:55.129
Andy Woodruff: All of these are cryptographically linked, giving us independently verifiable attribution.

115
00:13:55.370 --> 00:14:04.220
Andy Woodruff: This verifiability means we can eliminate the need for expensive teams of accountants and lawyers who just extract value away from the creator.

116
00:14:04.630 --> 00:14:05.620
Eric Drury (Forth Consulting): Then.

117
00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:09.640
Andy Woodruff: We layer on blockchain payments via USDC stablecoins.

118
00:14:10.020 --> 00:14:11.530
Andy Woodruff: for global payments.

119
00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:18.470
Andy Woodruff: Traditional rails charge 2-4% per transaction. With stablecoins, pennies.

120
00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:29.220
Andy Woodruff: For a creator earning $10,000 a month, that difference means keeping an extra $5,000 a year that would otherwise vanish to Stripe or PayPal.

121
00:14:30.230 --> 00:14:38.939
Andy Woodruff: With AI handling the heavy lifting, we keep headcount low across the board, not just at OCME, but with Next Artists and our other partners.

122
00:14:39.290 --> 00:14:46.210
Andy Woodruff: With less money flowing to overhead, more value flows from advertisers and subscribers directly to creators.

123
00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:56.390
Andy Woodruff: Only the minimum viable amount of value extraction occurs along the way, and critically, ecosystem participants vote on what that minimum is.

124
00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:00.330
Andy Woodruff: Here's where we're at today.

125
00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:07.110
Andy Woodruff: Our Trust Registry is live, with 600 members who have registered over 6,500 pieces of content.

126
00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:18.370
Andy Woodruff: I think this morning when I checked, it was about 6,900, so it's gone up since I wrote this a couple days ago. This is real, it's operating, and the growth has been remarkable.

127
00:15:18.740 --> 00:15:30.190
Andy Woodruff: Back in October of 2024, we had 12,000 views for the month. Our Halloween special peaked at 4,000 views in a single weekend, which felt significant at the time.

128
00:15:30.590 --> 00:15:36.279
Andy Woodruff: One year later, in October of 2025, we hit 5.1 million views for the month.

129
00:15:36.380 --> 00:15:39.790
Andy Woodruff: That's a 42,000% year-over-year growth.

130
00:15:40.080 --> 00:15:44.829
Andy Woodruff: We had 8 consecutive weeks exceeding 1 million views this fall.

131
00:15:45.190 --> 00:15:53.140
Andy Woodruff: While November fell slightly short of our viewership projections due to some weak viewership around Thanksgiving, our trajectory remains strong.

132
00:15:53.310 --> 00:16:00.029
Andy Woodruff: We went from 12,000 views in a month to routinely exceeding 1 million views per week.

133
00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:03.519
Andy Woodruff: This is a bottom-up adoption at scale.

134
00:16:03.770 --> 00:16:13.829
Andy Woodruff: Individual creators join because they see value, not because they're required to. And critically for this audience, it's built on the full Trust over IP stack.

135
00:16:16.500 --> 00:16:22.639
Andy Woodruff: Now, we'll dive into, how the ecosystem is structured to deliver on this growth.

136
00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:32.330
Andy Woodruff: The OCME is one non-profit, members-based ecosystem with multiple colonies or content verticals.

137
00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:41.140
Andy Woodruff: We built one internal structure to support a wide variety of digital content, rather than multiple ecosystems with external bridges.

138
00:16:41.720 --> 00:16:55.780
Andy Woodruff: At the center, you have the OCME as a neutral facilitator. We provide the trust layer, manage governance, and coordinate the ecosystem. We don't curate content, we don't build technology, and we don't compete with participants.

139
00:16:56.030 --> 00:17:01.120
Andy Woodruff: This structure supports multiple content verticals, or colonies as we call them.

140
00:17:01.870 --> 00:17:15.929
Andy Woodruff: Each colony may have different technical requirements, different content standards, and different distribution models, but they all operate within the same unified framework, leveraging the same identity system, the same trust layer.

141
00:17:16.160 --> 00:17:18.840
Andy Woodruff: And the same top-level governance principles.

142
00:17:19.359 --> 00:17:24.290
Andy Woodruff: Each colony has its own curators who license and distribute content.

143
00:17:24.410 --> 00:17:29.720
Andy Woodruff: As well as their own technology providers who build domain-specific tools.

144
00:17:30.020 --> 00:17:34.380
Andy Woodruff: But a creator joins the OCME once, they get one DID,

145
00:17:34.570 --> 00:17:40.219
Andy Woodruff: And can participate across any colony without managing multiple identities or credentials.

146
00:17:41.000 --> 00:17:52.069
Andy Woodruff: Over the next several slides, I'll walk through each component, the four key roles, how the value flows between them, what colonies actually are, and how governance coordinates it all.

147
00:17:54.740 --> 00:18:00.250
Andy Woodruff: The ecosystem has 4 distinct participants, each with defined revenue shares.

148
00:18:00.620 --> 00:18:04.730
Andy Woodruff: Creators are the foundation. They receive 60% of revenue.

149
00:18:05.020 --> 00:18:14.280
Andy Woodruff: They create the content, Music videos, news, gaming, or films, and retain ownership and control through their DIDs.

150
00:18:14.820 --> 00:18:23.520
Andy Woodruff: Without creators, there is no ecosystem. Importantly, this 60% is of gross revenue, or top line.

151
00:18:23.670 --> 00:18:28.210
Andy Woodruff: Platforms like YouTube and Spotify Payout and net revenue.

152
00:18:28.550 --> 00:18:34.559
Andy Woodruff: So where YouTube looks like they're close by paying out 55% of revenue, it's of net

153
00:18:34.750 --> 00:18:38.979
Andy Woodruff: So, our portion is noticeably higher.

154
00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:47.379
Andy Woodruff: With the OCME, creators know exactly what percentage of every dollar flows to them.

155
00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:57.030
Andy Woodruff: Curators license content from the registry, building audiences across distribution channels, and generating revenue.

156
00:18:57.550 --> 00:19:01.670
Andy Woodruff: For their work, they received 17.5% of gross revenue.

157
00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:09.820
Andy Woodruff: Today, AI Music Video Show is our founding curator, but other curators are welcome and encouraged.

158
00:19:11.300 --> 00:19:27.049
Andy Woodruff: AI Music Video Show is also available, 24-7 with their livestream, and they also stream on X on the weekends. So, with different curators, they can target different markets, different

159
00:19:27.370 --> 00:19:43.499
Andy Woodruff: niche content, with AI music video show. Most of our creators are leveraging the new AI, video and music generation tools, but very easily, we could have different,

160
00:19:43.630 --> 00:19:49.550
Andy Woodruff: genres or different regional niches, that spur up,

161
00:19:49.650 --> 00:19:57.380
Andy Woodruff: And we would love to have curators, working across, yeah, different regions, to…

162
00:19:57.700 --> 00:20:00.389
Andy Woodruff: Share the… all the great content.

163
00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:06.749
Andy Woodruff: Technology providers receive 17.5% for building the infrastructure.

164
00:20:06.780 --> 00:20:23.029
Andy Woodruff: NextArtists, for example, built the OCME Media Registry, the DID systems, and Creator Tools, all under a software licensing agreement with the OCME. They're an independent business, and the ecosystem is designed to support multiple technology providers.

165
00:20:23.730 --> 00:20:26.929
Andy Woodruff: some of the creator tools that Next Artist has provided.

166
00:20:27.110 --> 00:20:42.290
Andy Woodruff: Is our 24-7 live streaming technology, such that if a curator, wanted to join the ecosystem, but didn't have the, technology themselves to broadcast to where they wanted to.

167
00:20:42.290 --> 00:20:48.639
Andy Woodruff: We have next artist, they can leverage that technology, for that.

168
00:20:51.910 --> 00:20:53.210
Andy Woodruff: Let's see…

169
00:20:55.630 --> 00:21:11.450
Andy Woodruff: Once, so I just saw that Neil posted a link in the chat. If you drop the dev from the front of that and just go to ocmeco.org, that's our,

170
00:21:11.650 --> 00:21:12.560
Andy Woodruff: website.

171
00:21:16.100 --> 00:21:17.560
Andy Woodruff: And…

172
00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:34.729
Andy Woodruff: Yes, so, the final role here is the OCME, and they receive 5% as the neutral facilitator. We provide standards and a place to discuss governance. We operate membership services and treasury services.

173
00:21:35.520 --> 00:21:38.760
Andy Woodruff: And as a critical point, we do not curate content.

174
00:21:38.970 --> 00:21:45.079
Andy Woodruff: We do not build technology, and we do not compete with participants. We enable others to thrive.

175
00:21:45.580 --> 00:21:50.009
Andy Woodruff: Each role is independent, but coordinated through the governance framework.

176
00:21:55.900 --> 00:22:02.760
Andy Woodruff: So, here's a quick look at how the value can flow through the system.

177
00:22:03.150 --> 00:22:08.640
Andy Woodruff: coordinated by our did WebVHD schema.

178
00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:13.679
Andy Woodruff: So, first we have create. A creator uploads content.

179
00:22:13.930 --> 00:22:28.470
Andy Woodruff: Using tools built by technology providers, their creator DID is added to their content did, so they upload a piece of content, it gets an identifier. We also reference in that piece of content

180
00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:38.699
Andy Woodruff: It's media dids, as well as split sheets, and everything is cryptographically linked from the start. A split sheet is the piece of information that

181
00:22:38.970 --> 00:22:53.489
Andy Woodruff: holds, who gets paid and how much they get paid, for whenever this piece of content gets created. So if myself, if I was the only artist on the, piece of content, then it would just 100% would go to me.

182
00:22:53.490 --> 00:23:09.030
Andy Woodruff: But say Daryl and I did a collaboration, and we want to split it 50-50, whenever our piece of content makes $1, our system will automatically be able to assign 50 cents to me, and 50 cents to Daryl.

183
00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:12.850
Andy Woodruff: are

184
00:23:13.380 --> 00:23:23.699
Andy Woodruff: Step, curators discover content in the registry, so after a piece of content has been uploaded, curators will have access to that. They can find it in our registry.

185
00:23:23.900 --> 00:23:26.290
Andy Woodruff: From there, they create a playlist.

186
00:23:26.470 --> 00:23:38.599
Andy Woodruff: did, which contains all of the pieces of content, but specifically their content dates. And that way, the contents are, linked to what gets played, and

187
00:23:38.750 --> 00:23:41.210
Andy Woodruff: Critically linked back to the original creator.

188
00:23:42.610 --> 00:23:53.889
Andy Woodruff: For streaming, when playlists are streamed to audiences, a PlayRecordDid tracks every streaming event, referencing both the playlist and the curator, creating an immutable record.

189
00:23:54.840 --> 00:24:03.690
Andy Woodruff: For aggregation, added settlement, we aggregate all the play records to calculate total play minutes per creator and curator.

190
00:24:04.310 --> 00:24:20.430
Andy Woodruff: With this system, we're then able to bundle it all up and cryptographically validate it all, and then process the payments based on those splits.

191
00:24:20.780 --> 00:24:24.080
Andy Woodruff: So… and distribute?

192
00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:33.669
Andy Woodruff: We, distribute the revenue receipt DIDs, to collect income for the period. Payment record DIDs distribute

193
00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:39.060
Andy Woodruff: To… are distributed to creators and curators based on actual playtime.

194
00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:46.009
Andy Woodruff: And everything is transparent, verifiable, and there's no platform middlemen taking 30% off the top.

195
00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:59.220
Andy Woodruff: several of these pieces are still, in the works. We, are live today, but several of our, verification tools,

196
00:24:59.220 --> 00:25:08.740
Andy Woodruff: like the play records and revenue receipts. We are still, building out, and we'll be able to

197
00:25:08.740 --> 00:25:25.660
Andy Woodruff: hash blocks of play records and save them publicly, so that way any creator or anyone can verify that the revenue that flowed into the ecosystem was paid out in full to the people that it was supposed to be paid out to.

198
00:25:27.070 --> 00:25:32.709
Andy Woodruff: Part of this is, solving a critical problem in the…

199
00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:51.260
Andy Woodruff: record label industry, where about a billion dollars a year flows into, black box funds, basically fund. This is a fund that, the record labels don't know who to pay, where it goes unclaimed, and so the record labels just get to keep the money for themselves.

200
00:25:51.290 --> 00:26:06.439
Andy Woodruff: With this system, as… since the moment something comes in, it gets an identifier, and everything gets linked. When we go to pay people, there will not be content, who we do not know how to pay, or who it belongs to.

201
00:26:06.950 --> 00:26:17.210
Andy Woodruff: Creators could choose not to get paid, but then that's a… that's a choice that they're making, rather than, as part of our ecosystem, not knowing who that money belongs to.

202
00:26:18.060 --> 00:26:21.990
Andy Woodruff: This DID schema also makes automation possible.

203
00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:31.360
Andy Woodruff: Where we're able to verify attribution through every phase, and then automate this process, with very high confidence.

204
00:26:34.610 --> 00:26:38.350
Andy Woodruff: Colonies are content verticals within the OCME.

205
00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:44.850
Andy Woodruff: Music, videos, gaming, news media, and future verticals we may add.

206
00:26:45.480 --> 00:26:52.540
Andy Woodruff: They're not separate ecosystems. They're domain-specific verticals within one unified ecosystem.

207
00:26:52.960 --> 00:26:58.530
Andy Woodruff: The architecture is straightforward. A creator joins the OCME once, and gets one dead.

208
00:26:58.980 --> 00:27:01.560
Andy Woodruff: That identity works across every colony.

209
00:27:02.130 --> 00:27:05.050
Andy Woodruff: Music videos, gaming, and news.

210
00:27:05.360 --> 00:27:09.290
Andy Woodruff: The same identity, the same credentials, the same reputation.

211
00:27:09.390 --> 00:27:15.070
Andy Woodruff: All colonies share the same trust layer, the same governance framework, and the same identity system.

212
00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:22.729
Andy Woodruff: Each colony can have different technical requirements, different content standards, and different business models.

213
00:27:23.000 --> 00:27:28.330
Andy Woodruff: Gaming will have different needs for their infrastructure than news media will.

214
00:27:28.740 --> 00:27:33.270
Andy Woodruff: News will have different verification requirements than music videos.

215
00:27:33.480 --> 00:27:36.650
Andy Woodruff: Colonies handle that domain-specific complexity.

216
00:27:37.640 --> 00:27:48.469
Andy Woodruff: This was a very, interesting and kind of challenging thing to work through, with how do we support,

217
00:27:48.580 --> 00:27:54.759
Andy Woodruff: A large variety of different pieces of content, especially with

218
00:27:54.950 --> 00:27:59.270
Andy Woodruff: The way the media registry is set up, in that

219
00:27:59.270 --> 00:28:15.190
Andy Woodruff: As we described, you know, kind of through the value flow, while we did describe streaming, that streaming could have been replaced with a verified download, or a one-time purchase of a piece of content.

220
00:28:15.220 --> 00:28:20.920
Andy Woodruff: And so… We could support…

221
00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:24.660
Andy Woodruff: like we do today with MP4s and MOVs, but…

222
00:28:24.800 --> 00:28:40.799
Andy Woodruff: this could also support MP3s, or e-books, or digital poetry, as we have a couple people, interested in. And with that, how can we support those then on a…

223
00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:54.390
Andy Woodruff: Technical and trust layer inside of our governance framework and ecosystem without needing to kind of reinvent the ecosystem and governance every single time a new, you know, community wants to join.

224
00:28:56.760 --> 00:29:12.179
Andy Woodruff: Given our content vertical approach, we debated between a single ecosystem and an ecosystem of ecosystems approach. The ecosystem of ecosystems model, as described in the IRA white paper, is strictly heterarchical.

225
00:29:12.680 --> 00:29:24.160
Andy Woodruff: It's a flat structure where multiple separate trust registries exist as peers. Credentials issued in one ecosystem can't be trusted in another without a transition layer and bridges.

226
00:29:24.640 --> 00:29:28.310
Andy Woodruff: Creators manage multiple identities across different systems.

227
00:29:28.460 --> 00:29:34.340
Andy Woodruff: While this model will be effective for many, it was not the right fit for the OCME and our vision.

228
00:29:34.660 --> 00:29:43.149
Andy Woodruff: OCME takes a hierarchical approach, OCME sits at the top as the coordinating layer, with colonies underneath.

229
00:29:43.330 --> 00:29:46.990
Andy Woodruff: We're one ecosystem with a single unified trust layer.

230
00:29:47.510 --> 00:29:56.539
Andy Woodruff: our value flow and DID connections, as previously mentioned, whether the creator uploads a video, a game, or an e-book.

231
00:29:57.290 --> 00:30:00.889
Andy Woodruff: They don't need an account for each type of content they want to create.

232
00:30:01.160 --> 00:30:08.950
Andy Woodruff: Interoperability is built in because the colonies share the same foundation under OCME's coordination.

233
00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:15.379
Andy Woodruff: the hierarchy approach, Model gives us coherent governance.

234
00:30:15.510 --> 00:30:21.780
Andy Woodruff: One framework with colony-specific extensions, rather than competing systems trying to align.

235
00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:29.570
Andy Woodruff: It's simpler, more efficient, and better for creators who don't want to navigate the complexity across multiple ecosystems.

236
00:30:30.270 --> 00:30:33.749
Andy Woodruff: It also translates well into how we've structured our governance.

237
00:30:36.550 --> 00:30:44.429
Andy Woodruff: One thing that's been missing from all of these ecosystem slides is where governance actions tie into the ecosystem as a whole.

238
00:30:45.190 --> 00:30:48.579
Andy Woodruff: This happens through our Industry Advisory Council structure.

239
00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:56.890
Andy Woodruff: Today, we have one ecosystem OCME-wide IAC reviewing content… excuse me…

240
00:30:57.570 --> 00:31:10.169
Andy Woodruff: We have one IAC reviewing governance proposals, but as we expand to multiple colonies, we'll see the addition of colony-level IACs for handling domain-specific governance.

241
00:31:10.430 --> 00:31:19.500
Andy Woodruff: the intention of each IAC is to have a balance of creators, curators, technology providers, and industry leaders.

242
00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:26.010
Andy Woodruff: Their responsibility is to review proposals, maintain standards, and serve as stewards of the ecosystem.

243
00:31:26.350 --> 00:31:31.050
Andy Woodruff: To be a member of the IAC, you must also be a member of the OCME.

244
00:31:31.420 --> 00:31:35.700
Andy Woodruff: Governance actions are taken by stakeholders, not shareholders.

245
00:31:36.050 --> 00:31:40.480
Andy Woodruff: The people participating in the ecosystem are the ones governing it.

246
00:31:41.060 --> 00:31:48.740
Andy Woodruff: Gaming has different concerns than news media. Different content standards, different technical requirements, and different business models.

247
00:31:49.050 --> 00:31:58.910
Andy Woodruff: Colony IACs handle that domain-specific complexity, while the OCME-level IAC maintains coherence across the entire ecosystem.

248
00:32:02.180 --> 00:32:16.649
Andy Woodruff: So that's the ecosystem structure. It's modular, colonies can have different tech stacks, different curators, and different content types. But it's unified through one trust layer, one governance framework, and one identity system for creators.

249
00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:35.439
Andy Woodruff: The IAC structure coordinates universal principles at the OCME level, while giving colonies autonomy for domain-specific decisions. This architecture is designed to scale horizontally as we add more content verticals without recreating the trust infrastructure each time.

250
00:32:36.370 --> 00:32:45.890
Andy Woodruff: Now, let's dive into the governance architecture, how the framework is structured, who controls what, and how we maintain coherence as the ecosystem scales.

251
00:32:47.400 --> 00:33:02.570
Andy Woodruff: OCME's governance framework is built on the Trust Over IP governance metamodel. We're implementing a proven standard which gives us interoperability with other decentralized identity systems, and means we're speaking the same language as this community.

252
00:33:03.140 --> 00:33:14.779
Andy Woodruff: Most trust over IP metamodel implementations that I'm aware of work within a mature ecosystem. They build that complete framework based on existing rules and existing stakeholders.

253
00:33:15.140 --> 00:33:22.800
Andy Woodruff: We took an evolutionary approach, starting with a minimum viable ecosystem and growing as the ecosystem matures.

254
00:33:23.390 --> 00:33:26.119
Andy Woodruff: This can kind of be seen with our…

255
00:33:26.430 --> 00:33:34.639
Andy Woodruff: singular technology provider and our singular curator today, with the intention of expanding through more eco… more partners.

256
00:33:37.580 --> 00:33:48.989
Andy Woodruff: The metamodel structure is probably familiar to this audience. Here's how we've translated it into concrete governance documents, and critically, who controls changes at each level.

257
00:33:50.130 --> 00:33:57.220
Andy Woodruff: At Level 1, we have the Legal Foundation. The OCME is a 501C6 nonprofit.

258
00:33:57.970 --> 00:34:02.899
Andy Woodruff: And in order for us to make changes to this level of a charter.

259
00:34:03.000 --> 00:34:18.509
Andy Woodruff: We would need approval from the board, and federal-slash-state filings are required. This is very stringent and unlikely to change, and provides stability for ecosystem participants.

260
00:34:20.179 --> 00:34:29.410
Andy Woodruff: At Layer 2, we have the constitutional Framework, which is the primary document, and in our case, the OCME primary document.

261
00:34:30.050 --> 00:34:33.030
Andy Woodruff: In order for changes to be made to the primary document.

262
00:34:33.310 --> 00:34:38.420
Andy Woodruff: we would need the OCME IAC approval, as well as the board approval.

263
00:34:39.719 --> 00:34:56.750
Andy Woodruff: One thing to note is that we will store our list of colonies inside of our primary document, and so in order to add or maintain colonies, this kind of goes to the board level of approval.

264
00:34:59.720 --> 00:35:04.629
Andy Woodruff: The next layer down is our policy documents, or controlled documents.

265
00:35:05.900 --> 00:35:16.869
Andy Woodruff: Things that belong to a controlled document are any policies that impact members' rights, trust decisions, financial terms.

266
00:35:17.050 --> 00:35:21.060
Andy Woodruff: Legal obligations, or technical interoperability.

267
00:35:21.900 --> 00:35:27.660
Andy Woodruff: In order for us to make changes at this level, we would need approval from the OCME IAC.

268
00:35:30.150 --> 00:35:31.679
Andy Woodruff: In Layer 4 here.

269
00:35:31.950 --> 00:35:36.280
Andy Woodruff: This is where we get into the colony-specific governance.

270
00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:48.250
Andy Woodruff: leveraging the Trust over IP extensions model. So, the Trust over IP, metamodel allows for, extensions of your initial framework.

271
00:35:48.370 --> 00:35:53.359
Andy Woodruff: We will do this to support additional extensions per colony.

272
00:35:54.380 --> 00:36:13.610
Andy Woodruff: And that's in those colony extension documents, that's where we would define, how we define what is and is not the scope for each kind of colony, as well as, providing those colony, control documents around some of their different technical requirements.

273
00:36:13.720 --> 00:36:20.760
Andy Woodruff: And, additional, yeah, any decisions that are kind of colony-specific there.

274
00:36:22.320 --> 00:36:33.760
Andy Woodruff: we would have, to make changes to those documents, we would have approval from the Colony IAC, as well as a compliance review from the overall IAC.

275
00:36:35.230 --> 00:36:51.909
Andy Woodruff: In the final layer here, we have our operational procedures, or operational documents, which is outside of the scope of the Trust over IP metamodel, but this also includes things like SOPs, workflows, and implementation guides.

276
00:36:52.160 --> 00:36:58.129
Andy Woodruff: And in order to make changes to these, these are set by the OCME operations team.

277
00:37:00.980 --> 00:37:13.929
Andy Woodruff: Our minimum viable ecosystem right now, basically just uses the first three layers, so the, charter, a primary document, and some control documents. We are in the process of

278
00:37:14.080 --> 00:37:29.890
Andy Woodruff: writing out our, what we call Governance Framework 1.0, and that is the full colony-supported model, with the colony language, and structure, put into place.

279
00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:41.580
Andy Woodruff: For the documentation of this, we separated canonical governance from supporting materials using GitHub.

280
00:37:41.780 --> 00:37:45.450
Andy Woodruff: Our initial implementation was in Google Docs.

